task_4: (
identity- "bin.native_toolchain";
summary- "Create CE-OS-owned copies of the shell tools used underneath QPS terminal operations";
state- "ready";

product- "qps/bin owns an isolated modifiable command toolchain for search, text transformation, filesystem discovery, comparison, and terminal composition without modifying the host system commands";

current: (
item_1- "System rg, grep, sed, find, and related commands currently remain external host utilities";
item_2- "CE-OS should own copies that may be modified independently while preserving the original commands";
item_3- "probe, scope, scout, cv, and future terminal tools may use these owned binaries as replaceable mechanisms";
item_4- "Native command output often repeats identical file paths on every line, wasting context without adding evidence";
item_5- "CE-OS-owned commands should preserve useful native search and transformation behavior while compacting presentation for humans and constrained-context agents";
);

tools: (
search- "rg, grep";
transform- "sed, awk, cut, tr";
filesystem- "find";
stream- "cat, head, tail";
collection- "sort, uniq, xargs";
difference- "diff, patch";
);

findings: (
finding_1- "The host inventory shows rg, grep, sed, find, xargs, diff, and patch as standalone command paths; cat, head, tail, sort, uniq, cut, and tr resolve through the coreutils multicall binary; awk resolves through gawk";
finding_2- "Owning a command does not require changing its useful operation: CE-OS may retain the underlying implementation while replacing verbose presentation";
finding_3- "Repeated per-line path prefixes are presentation redundancy and should be collapsed into one path plus compact line or range evidence";
finding_4- "Compact rendering is part of the qps/bin command contract because terminal output is consumed by constrained-context local agents as well as humans";
finding_5- "The first owned rg keeps a byte-for-byte copy of the host ripgrep engine as rg.bin while qps/bin/native/rg owns CE-OS evidence presentation, separating matching semantics from rendering semantics";
finding_6- "The first compact-rg witness was invalid because it wrote search output inside the searched corpus, causing recursive self-matches; proof fixtures must keep corpus and generated evidence in separate directories";
finding_7- "Owned rg is proven: rg.bin is byte-identical to host ripgrep 15.2.0, matching output is equivalent on an isolated corpus, compact rendering preserves every matched physical line, repeated path output is reduced, and total output size is smaller";
finding_8- "Owned grep follows the same engine-versus-renderer boundary as rg; search-result compaction is a presentation concern while the copied host binary remains the matching authority";
finding_9- "The remaining regular shell mechanisms are now copied into qps/bin/native as owned engines; evidence-oriented tools are candidates for compact rendering while data, composition, and mutation tools preserve native output until a concrete CE-OS need is proven";
finding_10- "Termux coreutils is a multicall implementation selected by argv[0]; qps/bin therefore owns one coreutils.bin engine and correctly named cat, head, tail, sort, uniq, cut, and tr entrypoints rather than renamed independent binary copies";
finding_11- "Owned coreutils entrypoints now match host behavior for cat, head, tail, sort, uniq, cut, and tr after preserving the multicall argv[0] contract";
finding_12- "The final owned implementation is moving from copied host binaries to source-owned builds; host binaries remain behavioral parity references";
finding_13- "Upstream source is acquired under qps/bin/src at pinned revisions: coreutils bff0e5412, ripgrep 3fce3b5, sed 0c1fe22, grep e187ca5, findutils db41ebaf, gawk 6c7fd0eb2, diffutils ba490bf, patch a2b1922";
finding_14- "Source families cover the current tool substrate: coreutils owns cat/head/tail/sort/uniq/cut/tr, findutils owns find/xargs, with grep, sed, gawk, diffutils, patch, and ripgrep owning their corresponding commands";
finding_15- "The Pixel build environment now has clang 20.1.7, GNU Make 4.4.1, CMake 3.31.6, Autoconf 2.73, Automake 1.18.1, M4 1.4.19, Perl 5.42.2, Rust/Cargo 1.98.1, plus autopoint, bison, gettext, gperf, makeinfo, and texi2pdf";
finding_16- "Coreutils bootstrap completed successfully from the acquired development source tree and generated configure/build infrastructure";
finding_17- "The first out-of-tree Coreutils configure initially stopped at linker discovery; installing lld and compiler runtime support resolved the toolchain blocker";
finding_18- "Coreutils configure now completes successfully on the rooted Pixel using the Termux Clang/LLVM toolchain";
finding_19- "Direct leaf builds such as make src/cat do not reliably prepare every Coreutils BUILT_SOURCES prerequisite first; the intended top-level make correctly generates the gnulib/configuration substrate";
finding_20- "A full top-level Coreutils build completes successfully on the Pixel";
finding_21- "The resulting source-built cat reports GNU coreutils 9.11.270-bff0e and matches host cat output on the parity witness";
finding_22- "Ripgrep builds successfully from acquired source revision 3fce3b5 using Cargo and reports ripgrep 15.2.0";
finding_23- "Source-built ripgrep and host ripgrep produce the same four-match result set on an isolated corpus";
finding_24- "Raw ripgrep output order differs across builds because filesystem traversal order is not stable; matcher parity is defined by normalized result-set equivalence rather than incidental emission order";
finding_25- "GNU grep builds successfully from acquired source revision e187ca5 and reports grep 3.12.41-e187";
finding_26- "Source-built GNU grep and host grep produce the same four-match normalized result set on the isolated corpus";
finding_27- "GNU sed builds successfully from acquired source revision 0c1fe22 when configured with --without-selinux on the rooted Pixel";
finding_28- "Android exposes SELinux headers and libselinux but not every GNU userspace symbol sed expects, so header and library presence alone are insufficient capability evidence";
finding_29- "The sed configure probe also reports ACL support unavailable on this host; this is a non-fatal platform capability boundary for the current build";
finding_30- "The resulting source-built sed reports GNU sed 4.9.40-0c1f-dirty and matches host sed behavior on the transformation parity witness";
finding_31- "GNU findutils bootstrap and configure complete successfully from acquired development source revision db41ebaf on the rooted Pixel";
finding_32- "GNU findutils builds successfully from source and produces find and xargs 4.11.0.29-db41e-modified";
finding_33- "Source-built find matches host find on the isolated filesystem-selection witness after deterministic sorting";
finding_34- "Source-built xargs matches host xargs on the isolated argument-composition witness";
finding_35- "The gawk development checkout carried generated autotools metadata expecting aclocal-1.16 while the owned Pixel build environment provides a newer Automake toolchain";
finding_36- "Regenerating gawk autotools metadata with the current Autoconf, Automake, and Libtool toolchain succeeds, showing the versioned aclocal failure belongs to inherited build scaffolding rather than gawk language semantics";
finding_37- "Android Bionic provides fwrite_unlocked but gates its declaration by Android API surface, while gawk configure detected the linkable symbol and enabled HAVE_FWRITE_UNLOCKED";
finding_38- "A native symbol existing or linking successfully does not by itself prove that the symbol is declared and usable by the active compilation surface";
);

steps: (

step_1: (
goal- "Inventory the actual executable, symlink, script, multicall, and dynamic-library form of each host command";
product- "We know which tools can safely be copied directly and which require a different acquisition or build strategy";
state- "complete";
);

step_2: (
